From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Hold x264 under the bitrate limit

x264 was configured with "pass" 4 and the comment "constant bit rate
encoding". 4 is GST_X264_ENC_PASS_QUANT: a constant quantizer, which makes
x264 ignore both the bitrate and the VBV buffer. The bitrate limit, which
patch 0020 raised to 10 Mbit/s, never had any effect. Measured with the same
settings at 1080p30, a scrolling picture came out at 25 Mbit/s and a noisy one
at 390 Mbit/s, in frames of up to 1.8 MB. Over Wi-Fi Direct, where the radio
shares its time with another channel, such bursts fill the socket's send
buffer, and the backlog spreads back through the muxer to the sound queue:
sound breaks up or stops, and frames are dropped.

x264 now encodes at constant quality (pass 5, CRF at the default quantizer
21) under the bitrate limit through the VBV. A picture that fits within the
limit keeps the quality it had; one that does not is held to it. Measured:
scrolling 10 Mbit/s with frames up to 0.44 Mbit, noise 8 Mbit/s with frames
up to 0.49 Mbit, and the noisy picture encodes faster than before.

diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
@@ -704,7 +704,14 @@
 
       g_object_set (encoder,
                     "qos", TRUE,
-                    "pass", 4, /* constant bit rate encoding */
+                    /* Constant quality (x264's CRF, at the default quantizer)
+                     * held under the bitrate below by the VBV. This said
+                     * "constant bit rate", but 4 is GST_X264_ENC_PASS_QUANT,
+                     * a constant quantizer that ignores bitrate and VBV: a
+                     * scrolling page came out at 25 Mbit/s and a noisy
+                     * picture at 390 Mbit/s, in frames of up to 1.8 MB,
+                     * which no Wi-Fi link carries without backing up. */
+                    "pass", 5,
                     "tune", 0x4, /* zero latency */
                     "speed-preset", 1, /* ultrafast */
                     "rc-lookahead", 1,
